<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vyasa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Veda Vyasa, Krishna Dvaipayana, Vyāsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vyasa is a rishi, or sage, with a prominent role in most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> traditions, also known as Veda Vyasa or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> Dvaipayana. He is traditionally regarded as the author of the Mahābhārata, in which he also appears as a character, and Hindu tradition holds that he divided the single Veda into four and wrote the eighteen Purāṇas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> Sutras. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> he was the son of Satyavati and the sage Parasara, and at Satyavati's request he fathered Pāṇḍu and Dhṛtarāṣṭra. Many Hindus regard him as a partial incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and as one of the Chiranjivis, still alive in the Kali Yuga.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Indian tradition ascribes the Gita, like the Mahabharata, to the sage <b>Vyasa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The epic is traditionally ascribed to the sage <b>Vyasa</b>, who is also a major figure in it.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Puranic tradition, Ved <b>Vyasa</b> compiled the four Vedas and taught the Rigveda to Paila; an alternative version says Shakala compiled it from the teachings of the rishis.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
